  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nLight winds are in place across central and southeast Illinois \nthis afternoon, influenced by a surface high centered over \nnorth-central MO. Some high cloudiness associated with a weak \npositively-tilted short-wave trough over NE and KS is affecting \nthe region, but dry weather and slightly below normal \ntemperatures for early November persist.\n\nA fast-moving short-wave trough will transit across the Great \nLakes from tonight through Sunday afternoon, mainly bringing a \nband of high clouds through central Illinois Sunday morning but \nwith at least some chance of morning stratus, given nearly \nsaturated forecast soundings below about 2-3 kft. Otherwise, \nanticipate a shift in winds back to the west-northwest with an \nincrease in wind speeds on Sunday afternoon, but temperatures on\nSunday should be around 10 degrees warmer than they were today.\n\nOnce winds relax early Monday morning and temperatures cool,\nlook for fog potential to increase due to strong radiational \ncooling and saturated near-surface conditions. REFS member \nforecasts are currently split on whether visibilities below\none-half mile will develop, but the potential will need to be \nwatched. Forecast soundings are favorable, with a strong low \nlevel inversion and light wind speeds.\n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, anticipate steadily warming daytime temperatures\nand dry conditions through a period of zonal flow aloft. From \nTuesday night into Wednesday, the upper level pattern begins to \namplify with a strong ridge developing over the western US and a\ndeep trough being carved out east of that. The typical \nvariability exists at this time range regarding timing and \nplacement of individual short-wave troughs embedded in the flow \nand the relative strength of the main ridge/trough, but given \nthat every cluster has the same general pattern, confidence in \nthat is relatively high. Concurrently, there has been a modest \nincrease in precipitation chances for Wednesday as the initial \nwave makes its way into Illinois. Mean ensemble precipitable\nwater values are at around 0.6-0.8 inches, but the 90th \npercentile (10 percent chance of exceedance) is up to around 1 \ninch. Given the variability in placement/timing of individual \nshort waves, there remains only around a 20-30 percent chance of\ngreater than a quarter inch of total rainfall from early \nWednesday morning through early Thursday morning.\n\nThough precip potential remains on the modest side at this\npoint, we can be fairly confident in a notable cool-down late\nnext week after being largely in the 60s from Sunday through\nWednesday. The pattern in the latter half of next week indicates\nnortherly flow and perhaps multiple pushes of cool air. Though\nnothing highly unusual for mid-November is anticipated at this\npoint, look for a 50-80% chance of high temperatures below 50\ndegrees on Friday, with a 20-40% chance of high temperatures\nbelow 45 degrees. About half of the ensemble membership\ntranslates the ridge eastward and begins warming temperatures on\nSaturday next week, while the other half shows a persistent\ntrough over the eastern US and would likely continue colder\ntemperatures into next weekend.",
